      <h2 id="choosing-your-stack">Choosing Your Planner</h2>
      <p>Match the tool to the trip, not the other way around:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Solo or couple, want a full draft fast:</strong> Generate the itinerary with UTripla, verify restaurants and niche stops in Google Maps or TripAdvisor, book through Google Travel. One AI-native pass plus one verification layer.</li>
        <li><strong>Group or family trip:</strong> Wanderlog as the shared canvas, with everyone adding places and the AI suggestions filling gaps. A general assistant handles the research questions in the group chat.</li>
        <li><strong>Consensus-driven traveler:</strong> TripAdvisor's AI builder, because every suggestion carries review evidence you can read yourself.</li>
        <li><strong>Long or complex itinerary:</strong> A dedicated planner for the structure, an assistant for the edge questions, and Google Travel for booking — the three-layer stack most frequent travelers converge on.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>One principle across every stack: AI itineraries are drafts, not gospel. The tools are excellent at pacing, geography, and structure; they are weaker at knowing that a beloved café changed owners last spring. Spend your saved planning time verifying the parts of the plan that can ruin a day if wrong — opening hours, reservation policies, and anything you would genuinely regret missing. Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best AI travel planner in 2026?</summary>
          <p>It depends on how you travel. Google Travel is the best free foundation for flights, hotels, and destination research. Wanderlog is the best collaborative itinerary builder for group trips. UTripla is the strongest AI itinerary generator when you want a full personalized daily plan from your travel style, budget, and interests in one pass. ChatGPT works well as a research copilot for questions and rough drafts. Most frequent travelers combine two of these rather than relying on one.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can AI actually plan a good trip?</summary>
          <p>Yes for structure, with caveats for judgment. AI planners excel at building realistic daily schedules that respect opening hours, travel time between stops, and weather — work that used to take hours across a dozen browser tabs. The caveats: AI does not know a restaurant has gone downhill since its training data was collected, and generated plans tend toward famous highlights. Treat AI output as a strong draft, then layer in recent reviews and your own preferences.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are AI trip planners free?</summary>
          <p>Mostly. Google Travel, Wanderlog's core features, and TripAdvisor's trip builder are free, and general AI assistants like ChatGPT have capable free tiers. Specialized AI planners vary — some monetize through booking commissions rather than subscriptions. Check each tool's current pricing page before committing, since the AI travel category is still young and pricing changes often.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between an AI trip planner and a booking site?</summary>
          <p>Booking sites answer a known question: here is a route and dates, find the cheapest flight and hotel. AI trip planners answer an earlier one: given who you are and how many days you have, what should the trip look like? Planners produce the itinerary — days, neighborhoods, pacing, budget estimates — and some then hand off to booking. Many travelers use a planner first, then book through the platforms they already have loyalty status with.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

